\DOC LOCAL_SUB5_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB5_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal {A ?- eventually x86 P sN} past an inlined
modular-subtraction subroutine, swapping the assumptions about the current
state for ones about its successor and adding the arithmetic specification of
the computed result.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes an {int} (the same value in every observed trace) and a
{string list} of three names: the first names the variable introduced for the
subroutine's result, the remaining two name existing variables denoting the
operands whose difference is computed.
{
             A ?- eventually x86 P sN
      ====================================  LOCAL_SUB5_4_TAC n ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
             A' ?- eventually x86 P sN'
}
\noindent Exactly one subgoal results, and in the conclusion only the state
term changes, to a fresh successor state {sN'}. All assumptions mentioning the
old state — the program-counter equation, the {bytes_loaded} assumption, the
register and memory {read} equations, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame
relating the initial state to the current one — are discharged and replaced by
corresponding assumptions about the new state: the memory location written by
the subroutine now reads as the newly introduced result variable rather than
its previous contents, and the {MAYCHANGE} frame may be extended with further
components. The stale program-counter assumption is dropped and a new one for
the new state appears. Two new arithmetic assumptions about the result are
added: a bound on it, and a congruence equating it modulo the prime with the
difference of the two named operands. The pure arithmetic assumptions about the
other variables are carried over unchanged, although their order in the
assumption list may shift.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals of the above shape, with the same integer argument and a three-name
string list.

\ENDDOC
